TRUCKER BARBIE
by P.S. Wall (Off the Wall), August 1, 1999

"Ain't it just too cute for words," Loretta says as I pull myself up
into the cab of her 18-wheeler.

After flipping burgers at the truck stop for a year, it occurred to
Loretta that 28 1/2 cents per mile beat the heck out of minimum wage
plus tips.  And so she enrolled in trucker school.  Now, Loretta's new
home away from home is a plum purple, double-decker semi with built-in
fridge, TV and CD player.  It's Barbie's Doll House on 18 wheels.

"If Billy Ray Cyrus wuz sittin' there with his feet kicked up," Loretta
says, flipping open her lighter and firing up a Virginia Slim, "I'd
think I'd died and gone to heaven."

Grabbing a couple of Cokes in the little bottles out of the fridge,
Loretta knocks the tops off and passes one to me.

"Peanuts?"

"Think I will," I say.

Tearing the bag open with her teeth, Loretta shakes her peanuts into her
Coke.  Covering the top with her thumb, she gives it a shake, then
quickly chugs the foam.  If we had scones and clotted cream, the Queen
of England would feel right at home.

"Elvis," Loretta says, snapping her fingers and throwing her thumb
toward the rear, "hit the road."

Hopping off the passenger seat, Loretta's toy poodle, Elvis, prances
into the sleeping compartment.

"Ain't he just too cute for words," Loretta says, as we watch Elvis
strut past.

"Love your matching studded black leather jackets," I say.  About this
time it occurs to me that Loretta and Elvis could practically wear each
other's cloths.  Even in cowboy boots with 2-inch heels, Loretta can
barely see over the dash.

"Loretta," I finally have to ask, "how on earth do you drive this
thing?"

Giving me a grin, Loretta slides onto the driver's seat.  Suddenly
there's a hiss of air, and as if by magic, her seat automatically rises
to just the right height.

"Whoa!" I say.

Following her example, I hop into the passenger's seat.  Ssssst ... and
up I go.

"Technology -- the great equalizer," I say with awe.

"As good as Viagra," Loretta says knowingly.

After a few rides up and down on the seat, I continue my tour.  There
isn't a square inch of unadorned space.  Angels perch in the corners,
Saint Christopher swings from the rearview, and a plastic fluorescent
Jesus, arms spread like He's preaching the sermon on the dash, watches
over the cab.

Reaching up, I squeeze a little stuffed doll swinging from the overhead
light.  "You naughty boy!" the Smasher doll drones.  "Take it easy."

"Are those silk sheets?" I ask, peeking over my shoulder into the
sleeping cab.

"Sateen," Loretta says, patting the fuchsia sea of silk.  "Go on, give
her a try."

Head down, I crawl back into the sleeping cab, which Loretta has
decorated like I Dream of Jeannie's bottle.  Stretching out flat on my
back next to Elvis, I stare up at the mirror mounted above the bed.

No doubt there's some kind of OSHA regulation against mounting mirrors
on a truck roof, but at the moment I'm transfixed by my reflection.  I
look 15 pounds thinner, 10 years younger, and my roots don't show.  I
had no idea map lights could be so flattering.

"Wait a minute," I say, noticing that Elvis looks like a flea on the
pillow next to me, "is this one of those skinny mirrors?"

"Well," Loretta says, blowing cigarette smoke out the side vent, "let's
just say it does tend to put a man's ego into perspective."

Yeah, but I'm betting not half as much as a 98-pound female trucker.

