SMALL CARS NO MATCH FOR BIG RIGS
By Melvin Durai

Concerned about safety, the U.S. House of Representatives recently
approved a bill that would help keep Mexican trucks off American
highways.  That's a good move, but they now need to go a step further
and approve a bill that would help keep American trucks off American
highways.  Let them do all their hauling in Mexico and Canada, where
they have absolutely no chance of running over me.

Of course, I know it's not possible to ban trucks on the highway.  We
need all those trucks, partly because the economy is thriving and we're
making enough money to buy all kinds of things.  We need to have these
things delivered from afar, because the people who live near us are too
lazy to make anything good.  Their creativity usually stops at paper
planes, the kind that can stay in the air as long as a brick.  But who
am I to talk?  The only thing I've ever made is a big mess.

I'm concerned about trucks because I live near a highway called
Interstate 81, where, on a clear day, you can see thousands of trucks,
mostly tractor-trailers, many driving faster than Jeff Gordon.  I don't
know what all those truckers are hauling or where they're going
(probably Wal-Mart), but I really wish they'd take at least one day a
week off.  That way, I could do all my driving on that day.

It scares me when I'm driving my small Nissan Sentra on the highway and
a huge tractor-trailer, bigger than my apartment, is following me so
close that I can smell the driver's breath.  Considering all the bumps
on I-81, the trucker could probably run over me and not even notice.
Imagine the conversation when a cop pulls him over:

TRUCKER:  Officer, what's the problem?  Was I speeding?

OFFICER:  No, you were only doing 90.  But did you know that you just
flattened a Sentra about a mile back?

TRUCKER:  A Sentra?  Is that some kind of animal?  Oh no, I'm going to
be in trouble with those animal activists again, aren't I?

OFFICER:  Calm down.  A Sentra is a car and there were no animals
aboard, only an Asian dude.

TRUCKER:  Phew.  What a relief.  Is the dude all right?

OFFICER:  He's fine.  He'll just be in a coma for a few months.  But
given proper care, he should regain normal brain activity.  Especially
if we can cut him out of the wreckage within a day or two.

TRUCKER:  Does this mean I'll lose my driving privileges?

OFFICER:  No, you're lucky.  The victim didn't get a chance to call your
"How's my driving?" number.  By the time the number was in front of him,
he was already unconscious.

To be fair, most truckers are good, sensible drivers -- better than the
average motorist.  But many are under pressure to speed and exceed the
federal driving limit of 60 hours a week.  When they run into a car (no
matter who's at fault), guess who gets killed.  It's like Jesse Ventura
running into Calista Flockhart.

More than 5,000 people die in big-truck accidents every year and fewer
than 5% are truckers.  It's not unusual to hear about an accident that
kills several people in a car, but leaves the trucker with barely a
scratch.

So if you're concerned about your family's safety on the highway, here's
my advice:  withdraw all your money from the bank and invest it in a
shiny new tractor-trailer.

You may have trouble making turns or parallel parking, but you'll still
have lots of fun, cruising down the highway, entertaining your children
and scaring people like me.
